WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:06.270
If the basic technical ideas behind deep learning, behind neural networks, have been around for

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.310
decades, why are they only just now taking off?

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:13.950
In this video, let's go over some of the main drivers behind the rise of deep learning,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.190
because I think this will help you better spot the best opportunities within your own

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.710
organisation to apply these two.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.430
Over the last few years, a lot of people have asked me, Andrew, why is deep learning

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.550
certainly working so well?

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.430
And when I'm asked that question, this is usually the picture I draw for them.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:35.790
Let's say we plot a figure where on the horizontal axis, we plot the amount of data

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.910
we have for a task.

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:43.550
And let's say on the vertical axis, we plot the performance of our learning algorithm,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:50.790
such as the accuracy of our spam classifier or our ad-click predictor, or the accuracy of

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:56.310
our neural net for figuring out the position of other calls for our self-driving car.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.710
It turns out if you plot the performance of a traditional learning algorithm, like

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:06.710
support vector machine or logistic regression, as a function of the amount of data you have,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.350
you might get a curve that looks like this, where the performance improves for a while

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.190
as you add more data.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.750
But after a while, the performance pretty much plateaus.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.590
This is supposed to be a horizontal line to draw that very well.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:26.870
It was as if they didn't know what to do with huge amounts of data.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.430
And what happened in our society over the last 20 years maybe is that for a lot of

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:38.390
problems, we've went from having a relatively small amount of data to having often a fairly

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.190
large amount of data.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.910
And a lot of this was thanks to the digitization of a society where so much

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.950
human activity is now in the digital realm.

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:53.070
We spend so much time on our computers, on websites, on mobile apps, and activities on

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.590
digital devices creates data.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.790
And thanks to the rise of inexpensive cameras built into our cell phones,

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:06.950
accelerometers, all sorts of sensors in the Internet of Things, we also just have been

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.830
collecting more and more and more data.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.110
So over the last 20 years, for a lot of applications, we just accumulated a lot more

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:18.750
data, more than traditional learning algorithms were able to effectively take

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.070
advantage of.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:26.310
And with neural networks, it turns out that if you train a small neural net, then its

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.070
performance maybe looks like that.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:34.710
If you train a somewhat larger neural net, let's call this a medium-sized neural net,

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.710
its performance often even a little bit better.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:42.230
And if you train a very large neural net, then its performance often just keeps

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:43.870
getting better and better.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.430
So a couple observations.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.150
One is if you want to hit this very high level of performance, then you need two

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:50.710
things.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:56.510
First, often you need to be able to train a big enough neural network in order to

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:59.030
take advantage of a huge amount of data.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.750
And second, you need to be out here on the x-axis.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.310
You do need a lot of data.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.710
So we often say that scale has been driving deep learning progress.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:13.230
And by scale, I mean both the size of the neural network, meaning just a neural

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:18.190
network with a lot of hidden units, a lot of parameters, a lot of connections, as

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.150
well as scale of the data.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.430
In fact, today, one of the most reliable ways to get better performance in a neural

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:30.510
network is often to either train a bigger network or throw more data at it.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.710
And that only works up to a point because eventually you run out of data or

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:37.670
eventually the neural network is so big that it takes too long to train.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:42.350
But just improving scale has actually taken us a long way in the world of

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:43.510
deep learning.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:47.870
In order to make this diagram a bit more technically precise, let me just add a

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:48.870
few more things.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.630
I wrote the amount of data on the x-axis.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:58.750
Technically, this is amount of labeled data, where by labeled data, I mean

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:03.030
training examples, we have both the input x and the label y.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.870
I want to introduce a little bit of notation that we'll use later.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.710
In this course, we're going to use lowercase alphabet M to denote the size of

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:12.710
our training set.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.870
So the number of training examples is lowercase m, so that's the

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.270
horizontal axis.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:20.390
A couple other details to this figure.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:26.430
In this regime of small training sets, the relative ordering of the

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.370
algorithms is actually not very well defined.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:33.390
So if you don't have a lot of training data, it's often up to your

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.150
skill at hand engineering features that determines performance.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:42.630
So it's quite possible that if someone training an SVM is more motivated

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:46.310
to hand engineer features than someone training an even large neural

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:50.910
net, maybe in this small training set regime, the SVM could do better.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.110
So in this region to the left of the figure, the relative ordering

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.310
between the algorithms is not that well defined.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.190
And performance depends much more on your skill at hand

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.710
engineering features and other little details of the algorithms.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:10.070
And as only in this big data regime, very large training sets, very large M

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.950
regime in the right, that we more consistently see large neural nets

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:17.450
dominating the other approaches.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.430
And so if any of your friends ask you why are neural nets taking off, I

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:25.310
would encourage you to draw this picture for them as well.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:29.430
So I would say that in the early days in the modern rise of deep learning,

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:33.870
it was scale of data and scale of computation.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:38.750
So it was our ability to train very large neural networks, either a CPU or a GPU

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.910
that enabled us to make a lot of progress.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:45.390
But increasingly, especially in the last several years, we've been seeing

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.070
tremendous algorithmic innovation as well.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:51.790
So I also don't want to understate that.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.870
Interestingly, many of the algorithmic innovations have been about trying

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.150
to make neural networks run much faster.

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.950
So as a concrete example, one of the huge breakthroughs in neural networks

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:11.310
has been switching from a sigmoid function, which looks like this, to a

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:16.870
relu function, which we talked about briefly in an earlier video that looks

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.390
like this.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.190
If you don't understand the details of what I'm about to say, don't worry

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:25.190
about it, but it turns out that one of the problems of using sigmoid

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:28.790
functions in machine learning is that there are these regions here where

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:31.910
the slope of the function, where the gradient is nearly zero.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:36.070
And so learning becomes really slow because when you implement gradient

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:40.190
descent and the gradient is zero, the parameters just change very slowly.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:41.830
And so learning is very slow.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:46.430
Whereas by changing the, what's called the activation function of a neural

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:51.910
network to use this function called the relu function or the rectified

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:56.310
linear unit, RELU, the gradient is equal to one for all positive

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:58.070
values of input, right?

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:03.150
And so the gradient is much less likely to gradually shrink to zero.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:06.950
And the gradient here, the slope of this line is zero on the left, but it

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:11.470
turns out that just by switching to the sigmoid function, to the relu

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:16.270
function has made an algorithm called gradient descent work much faster.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:20.910
And so just an example of a maybe a relatively simple algorithm innovation,

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.670
but ultimately the impact of this algorithm innovation was it really

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.510
helped computation.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.470
So there've actually been quite a lot of examples like this where we

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.870
change the algorithm because it allows our code to run much faster.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.470
And this allows us to train bigger neural networks or to do so in a

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.790
reasonable amount of time, even when we have a large network or a lot of data.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:48.350
The other reason that fast computation is important is that it turns

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.270
out the process of training your network is very iterative.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.430
Often you have an idea for a neural network architecture.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:00.150
And so you implement your idea in code, implementing your idea.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.750
Then as you run an experiment, which tells you how well your neural network

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:08.230
does, and then by looking at it, you go back to change the details of your

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:12.590
neural network, and then you go around this circle over and over.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:17.630
And when your neural network takes a long time to train, it just takes a

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.030
long time to go around this cycle.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:24.750
And there's a huge difference in your productivity, building effective neural

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:31.150
networks, when you can have an idea and try it and see if it worked in 10

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:36.310
minutes or maybe at most a day versus if you have to train your neural

00:08:36.310 --> 00:08:41.830
network for a month, which sometimes does happen because we get a result

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:45.430
back, you know, in 10 minutes or maybe in a day, you could just try a

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:49.630
lot more ideas and be much more likely to discover a neural network

00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.350
that works well for your application.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:58.790
And so foster computation has really helped in terms of speeding up the rate.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:01.350
I wish you can get an experimental result back.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:06.670
And this has really helped both practitioners of neural networks as well

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:12.150
as researchers working on deep learning iterate much faster and improve

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:13.950
your ideas much faster.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:18.790
And so all this has also been a huge boon to the entire deep learning

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:23.630
research community, which has been incredible at just inventing new

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:26.990
algorithms and making nonstop progress on that front.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:31.510
So these are some of the forces powering the rise of deep learning.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.870
But the good news is that these forces are still working powerfully

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:37.630
to make deep learning even better.

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:38.790
Take data.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.990
Society is still throwing off more and more digital data.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.670
Or take computation with the rise of specialized hardware like GPUs and

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.190
faster networking, many types of hardware.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:53.310
I'm actually quite confident that our ability to build very large neural

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.950
networks from a shared computation point of view will keep on getting better.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:01.790
And take algorithms while the whole deep learning research community is still

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:06.150
continuously phenomenal at innovating on the algorithms front.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.030
So because of this, I think that we can be optimistic, I'm certainly

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:14.070
optimistic that deep learning will keep on getting better for many years to come.

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.430
So with that, let's go on to the last video of this section, where we'll talk a

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:20.790
little bit more about what you learn from this course.
